WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.717 --> 00:00:07.050
KIKI MARCONDES: Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.470
And welcome to
IPG Online Meetup.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:11.640
My name is Kiki.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.050
And I'll be your host today.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.640
We're broadcasting live from
Google London headquarters.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.990
And we are all very excited
to have you joining us today.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:26.550
Back by popular demand, today
marks the eighth IPG Online

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.080
Meetup edition.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.890
And our goal is to be as
interactive and educational

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.490
as possible.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.880
So in today's
session, we are joined

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.790
by Oliver Ogg and Paul Rohan.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:42.450
And we will talk about
driving banking innovation

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.850
in the Age of Assistance.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:50.490
So first, Ollie talking
about FIS disruption.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:55.290
We're going to have Paul Rohan
talking about the role of APIs

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.640
in banking.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.490
And then we're going to
go to a fireside chat.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.790
As always, we will provide you
with plenty of opportunities

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:08.390
for participating
and asking questions.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.620
So before we start,
few reminders.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.390
Please ask questions
throughout the session.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:18.400
We will have a dedicated time
to answer to all the questions

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.780
so feel free to submit
them at any time.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:27.130
And also to ask questions,
use the link here

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.780
or click on the Ask Question
button on your watch page.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.570
Now I would like to
welcome to the stage

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.360
our special guest Paul Rohan.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.750
Paul is an author of two
books on open banking

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.710
and has a vast experience
working with C-suite

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:49.360
on the area around the
world and also Oliver Ogg.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.630
So without further
ado, grab a drink,

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.570
make yourself comfortable,
and let's get started.

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.130
Over to you, Ollie.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:58.750
OLIVER OGG: Thanks, Kiki.

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.950
So over the last
couple of weeks,

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.880
I've just been keeping an eye
on those that have registered

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.260
for this online meetup.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.340
So welcome.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.543
But I see we've got
a lot from the world

00:02:08.543 --> 00:02:09.460
of financial services.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.440
But we do also have
quite a few who

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.960
don't directly, at
least today, work

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.370
in the world of
financial services.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.340
So I thought it'd be a
good opportunity just

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.990
to take a holistic view across
what we see in the world of FIS

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:25.660
disruption and see
some key themes that I

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.390
think are relevant to all.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:32.950
So first of all, the
concept of being open.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:37.150
So the fintechs, the digital
natives are, by default,

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.340
as part of their DNA, open.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:43.930
They expose their core data,
their core services via APIs

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:49.150
so that it is extremely easy
for others to work with them.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.280
That's quite different
from the incumbents.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:57.310
So ask today during this meetup
what is right for the business

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.550
that you work in and
what's the appetite today.

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.920
It could be a significant shift.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:05.770
Second, leveraging data.

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.320
If you work for an
incumbent, you are no doubt

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.560
sitting on an extraordinary
wealth of data

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.530
that I'm sure is not
being fully leveraged.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:17.170
Often, there are
significant challenges

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.210
around data being in silos.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:24.430
So at least internally,
expose this valuable data

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:27.730
set within the business so that
other parts of the business

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.840
can consume it easily.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.790
But also, think about
these new data products

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:37.630
that you could surface up as
APIs to external participants.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:39.470
These can be
incredibly valuable.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.380
Think about the
insights that you

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:48.200
can derive from your scale and
the data on which you sit on.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.430
Third, gravitational pull.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:54.650
So why would others
want to work with you?

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.440
Now it could be that
it's incredibly easy

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:58.530
to work with you.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:02.270
It could be your scale,
perhaps the number of customers

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:05.605
or the number of products and
services that you offer today.

00:04:05.605 --> 00:04:07.730
Perhaps it's the unique
insight that you've managed

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.950
to derive out of your data.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:15.320
It could well be a combination
of a number of things.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.420
To think about what
is the unique value

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:21.920
that you can offer
to attract others.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.580
Your traditional
ego is certainly not

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:29.610
going to be enough
going forwards.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.060
Fourth, cross-industry
ecosystem.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.750
So we see this for two main
reasons, the first being

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:38.060
about maintaining relevance.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.820
So for many businesses
today, in order

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:44.360
to maintain a continual
relevance to their customers,

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:47.180
they are looking to
bring additional products

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:50.450
and services from other
industries to the channels

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:53.090
that they own that
they can drive down

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:56.660
to their existing customer
base so that, as a customer,

00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:00.890
I'm more continually engaging
with that particular brand.

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:03.290
Secondly, we see
it when an industry

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:06.890
is seeing some level of
disruption and the more severe

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:09.590
the disruption, the more
cross-industry collaboration

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:11.240
we observe.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:15.530
And this is a defensive
and offensive play

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:18.860
in a competitive landscape.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:21.560
So take a step back and
consider your organization's

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:25.460
own competitive landscape
and, again, the appetite

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:29.180
of this cross-industry
ecosystem play.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:32.910
And last but not least,
the ability to execute.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:37.010
So there are lots of
challenges in doing this well.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:39.620
There will be organizational
mindset issues.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:41.450
There will be leadership issues.

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:44.150
There will be
organizational structures,

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:46.550
personal egos in the way.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:48.240
So this is not a hard--

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:48.740
sorry.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:52.280
This is not an easy thing
to immediately solve.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:57.140
And of course, talent is
hard to attract and maintain.

00:05:57.140 --> 00:05:59.210
So these are five
very common themes

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.790
that we see in the world
of financial services

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:03.860
but certainly in
other industries

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.830
and hopefully will be useful to
have in the back of your mind

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:10.250
as we explore this
topic in more depth.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:11.300
Thanks very much.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:12.710
Over to you, Paul.

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:14.430
PAUL ROHAN: Thank you.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:15.590
Thank you, Ollie.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:17.670
And good morning, everyone.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:20.270
I'm going to talk about the
role of APIs in banking.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:25.220
And while a lot of my comments
will be specific to banking,

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.070
they can probably apply
to any really traditional

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.920
mature business that's been
around a long time that

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:34.160
wasn't born on the internet and
is now seeing both the threats

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.670
and opportunities from
digital ecosystems.

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:42.055
So I have to cover four
themes today, four topics.

00:06:42.055 --> 00:06:43.680
And I'm going to just
call them out now

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.870
to give you a chance to consider
them at a very high level

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.030
and maybe perhaps contribute
any questions that you

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:50.550
have to our discussion.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.080
We have a fantastic
vantage point

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.140
at Google Apigee in
that not only do we

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.600
serve a lot of banks and
financial services companies

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.560
globally but we work
across every vertical.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:02.310
And we think there's
something much bigger

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:05.610
happening than just banking or
open banking, which we would

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:08.610
call the Age of Assistance,
which I'll come on and describe

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:10.830
in a bit more
detail but we think

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.220
to be relevant, and
responsive, and fast in the Age

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.450
of Assistance.

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:18.670
API products aren't
just an optional extra.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:19.170
Excuse me.

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:22.200
We think they're
absolutely essential.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:25.170
Secondly, the world
of digital ecosystems

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:29.813
are radically different in terms
of how people think and behave

00:07:29.813 --> 00:07:31.230
in terms of growing
their business

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:33.600
and managing their business
strategy, and technology,

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:35.520
and managing their brands.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.130
And there's lots of
discussion around mindset

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:40.068
and corporate culture.

00:07:40.068 --> 00:07:41.610
But to be really
explicit here, we're

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:45.480
saying we think work is required
on both computer systems

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:47.220
and belief systems.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:50.110
Sounds a bit odd but
come on to discuss

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:51.312
that in a bit more detail.

00:07:51.312 --> 00:07:52.770
And we think it's
insufficient just

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:54.810
to do work on your
computer systems

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:57.300
and not work on
your belief systems.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.360
We think this will prevent you
scaling or certainly getting

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:04.290
the full benefits of
digital ecosystems.

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:06.090
Then I think brand
is very important,

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:09.150
brand strategy, for a couple of
reasons-- for several reasons,

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:09.900
in fact.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.330
Firstly, brand strategy
has a much longer duration

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:15.702
than business strategy
or technology strategy.

00:08:15.702 --> 00:08:17.910
So your technology strategy
or your business strategy

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:21.120
might have one or two years to
run whereas brand strategy is

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.660
very long term 5 to
10 year thinking what

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:26.790
type of emotional
reaction and response

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:29.610
do we want to get from the
marketplace, far broader

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:33.150
than just our specific
products and services.

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:37.049
Often people who are from a
pure technology background

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.659
say to me, banks are
in terrible trouble

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:41.820
or the banking industry
is in terrible trouble.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:45.330
Their user interfaces, the
user experiences aren't good.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:48.090
They must be out of
business in a few years.

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:50.790
And my response
is, well, we need

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.520
to reflect on that because
the banking industry, based

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:57.420
on which historian you believe,
could be up to 4,000 years old.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:02.190
So it has thrived and survived
many evolutions in technology.

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:04.770
So we have a view
from much observation

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.400
that maybe the brand
purpose of financial brands

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:12.660
won't be changed hugely
by digital ecosystems.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:17.500
However, brand audiences will be
changed in a very radical way.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:21.120
And then lastly, what will
be a great financial brand

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.730
experience in the era
of digital ecosystems

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:26.070
and in the Age of Assistance?

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:31.650
And we think that the financial
brands that treat developers

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:34.050
as customers and
approach them entirely

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:35.880
as they would
approach customers are

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:40.370
the most likely to scale
and succeed in this world.

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:43.880
So firstly, what do we mean
by the Age of Assistance?

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:47.090
And in the context
of that, we think

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:49.290
it's very much a
hyperconnected world.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:52.430
And we would define
this as that it's

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:55.340
impacting all services, not
just financial services,

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:58.220
and that customers are now
expecting their favorite brands

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:00.560
to work together to provide
connected experiences

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:02.730
in digital ecosystems.

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:04.490
And if that sounds
a bit far away

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:08.030
or theoretical for a traditional
financial services company,

00:10:08.030 --> 00:10:11.420
you need to consider
many aspects of this

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:14.510
but focus in on
customer journeys.

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:18.530
So how does your company
define customer journeys?

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:21.320
Is it a long time since you've
reflected on what they mean?

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:23.930
Do you mean customer
journeys by they

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.420
knock on the door of
your store or your branch

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:29.870
or they knock on the door of
your proprietary digital app

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:32.690
and then customer journey
management is ushering them

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:36.950
to the right solution, the
right product to consider,

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:38.660
the suitable service
that you have

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:41.090
for that stage of
their life cycle?

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:44.870
The big concern for companies
that are adopting that mindset

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:46.790
is that there's a new
generation of buyers,

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:51.680
whether they are young
consumers or young businesses,

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:54.090
who are on a journey
to a digital ecosystem.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:57.020
And they expect their favorite
brands and favorite providers

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:59.180
to collaborate and
provide a connected end

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:01.350
to end experience.

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:04.220
So in reality, they're on a
journey through the ecosystem.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:06.020
And if you wish to
serve them and remain

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:08.060
relevant and one of
their favorite brands,

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:11.270
you need to push your services
out there into that environment

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:14.390
and let them use those services
through whichever interface

00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:18.330
is suitable, and
appropriate, and contextual.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:22.070
So in that context, rethinking
what you mean by a customer

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:25.340
journey and focusing in
on the young generation

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:27.530
and how they see
customer journeys

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:32.390
causes a major rethink of
not just your brand strategy

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:35.420
but also your business strategy,
your business architecture,

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:38.000
your technical strategy
and, if all of these things

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:40.460
are going to work, your mindset.

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:42.080
And this mindset
needs to be shared

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:44.915
right across the organization
across all key roles

00:11:44.915 --> 00:11:46.040
from the top to the bottom.

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:54.520
We talk to many banks about
digital transformation.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:58.120
And often their focus
can be quite narrow.

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:00.820
And they believe that
transformation involves

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:03.820
using new technologies,
and doing new things,

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.243
and doing things in new ways.

00:12:06.243 --> 00:12:08.410
And often we have to remind
them that transformation

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:10.520
is a combination of two things.

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:14.770
It's both starting the
new ways, doing new things

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:18.160
with new technologies, but
also effectively stopping

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:20.680
the old ways, stopping
using the old technologies,

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:22.600
stop using the old methods.

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:24.910
And it's useful to consider
the Age of Assistance

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.710
from this perspective.

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:30.070
So we would argue very strongly
that, in the Age of Assistance,

00:12:30.070 --> 00:12:34.000
only API enabled collaboration
is going to help you keep up

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:35.960
and is going to be relevant.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:37.660
So look at some of
the ways in which

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:40.420
a traditional financial
services company or indeed

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:43.733
any traditional
organization might partner.

00:12:43.733 --> 00:12:45.400
And they would have
historically focused

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:48.640
on things like joint ventures
or strategic alliances.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:51.700
And a joint venture
is incredibly precise

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.470
and that a separate
organization is set up to do it.

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:56.380
But it's plagued with problems.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:12:58.550
It's very slow to get together.

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:01.270
You can have a clash of cultures
inside the new organization

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:03.700
from people from two
different organizations.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:05.950
It takes an incredible
amount of preplanning.

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:10.090
And if it doesn't work out,
it's very hard to exit.

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:12.370
Strategic alliances
aren't quite as rigid,

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:14.110
but they're very, very loose.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:16.900
And they're slow
to get to market.

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:19.690
You're assembling projects
inside both organizations.

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:21.940
And it's tough enough to
assemble a project inside one

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:25.360
organization but to collaborate
across two organizations

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:28.120
using projects is incredibly
difficult. So very hard

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:31.630
to coordinate and ambiguous
about who's doing what.

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:33.430
And the crucial flaw
that both of them

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:36.280
share apart from being
slow, or rigid, or loose

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:38.000
is that it's not reusable.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.150
It's specific to
that collaboration

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:43.000
whereas API enabled
partnering, the investment

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:46.148
is reusable so you try out
an API with one partner.

00:13:46.148 --> 00:13:48.190
Turns out to be a good
API but the wrong partner.

00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:50.020
Move swiftly onto the next one.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:51.460
It's fast to market.

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:53.650
There's automated
processes behind the APIs

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:55.450
which everyone can trust.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:57.400
There's automated
fast onboarding.

00:13:57.400 --> 00:14:00.310
Companies that go
partnering use the APIs move

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:04.060
from onboarding of partners
that last months into onboarding

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:06.370
a partner that lasts hours.

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:08.290
And there's no barriers to exit.

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:10.060
The investment is reusable.

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:12.430
You can move on to other
partnerships, and other ideas,

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:14.830
and other collaborations
incredibly quickly.

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:16.390
And within this
world, you can come

00:14:16.390 --> 00:14:19.600
across unanticipated
opportunities with your APIs

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.235
that no one in your organization
could have imagined before you

00:14:22.235 --> 00:14:23.110
published those APIs.

00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:29.440
So we have a strong view
about the Age of Assistance.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:32.470
We think it straddles all
verticals, not just banking.

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:35.860
So we sell API
management software.

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:36.970
You buy our software.

00:14:36.970 --> 00:14:38.500
The problem is sorted.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:39.530
That's not the case.

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:42.072
We think there's a lot more to
be done than changing computer

00:14:42.072 --> 00:14:43.810
systems to handle
APIs if you wish

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:48.980
to be successful in this
world as a financial brand.

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:50.840
So on to belief systems.

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:53.840
And we like to think
that we are incredibly

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:55.550
clinical in our
business environment,

00:14:55.550 --> 00:15:00.000
that we make every cold blooded,
analytical economic decisions.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.390
And that is the case,
but those decisions

00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:05.960
are built upon a base of
assumptions and beliefs

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:09.470
about how the marketplace works,
how to grow in the marketplace,

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:11.600
how to present services
to the marketplace,

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.037
how to segment the marketplace.

00:15:14.037 --> 00:15:15.620
The crucial thing
about belief systems

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:19.190
is that they are systems that
people believe all elements

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:21.080
of the system, not just one.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.750
It's not a pick and mix
where they can select.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:28.250
So in that context, we
think the Age of Assistance

00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:30.800
and the digital ecosystems
that are serving

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:33.890
in the Age of Assistance does
require quite a radically

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:37.400
different belief system, a
set of beliefs that are not

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:39.920
only relevant and
effective in isolation

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:44.060
but all connect to each other
and that these beliefs drive

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:46.580
a common set of
behaviors and assumptions

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:48.590
right across the
organization, which

00:15:48.590 --> 00:15:51.860
means you can scale
cohesively whatever technology

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:53.990
strategy you're pursuing
or whatever business

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.920
strategy you're pursuing.

00:15:56.920 --> 00:16:00.400
So for fun, this hearkens
back to those old TV

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:02.800
ads for soap powder.

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:05.510
Do you prefer
brand A or brand B?

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:07.870
But let's consider financial
brand A and financial brand

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:10.870
B that are looking at
the world of ecosystems.

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:13.450
Let's hypothesize that
brand has bought themselves

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:16.480
a brand spanking new computer
system, an API management

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:18.310
platform for ecosystems.

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:21.220
But there hasn't been a
general discussion and revision

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:23.093
of the belief system.

00:16:23.093 --> 00:16:24.010
Why are we doing this?

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:25.427
How's the market
going to operate?

00:16:25.427 --> 00:16:27.190
How should we present ourselves?

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:29.290
And then brand B,
which is not only

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:32.222
working on its computer systems
but is having a long, and deep,

00:16:32.222 --> 00:16:34.180
and broad discussion from
the top to the bottom

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:37.720
of the organization about
what they should believe

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:40.420
about ecosystems and how this
is going to guide our decision

00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:42.970
making.

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:45.960
So a simplified but we
think effective summary

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:47.202
of two belief systems--

00:16:47.202 --> 00:16:49.410
you can tell we have the
one we like in green and one

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:51.210
we're not so sure about in red.

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:55.140
But the way to read the
slide is down in sequence.

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:57.600
So belief systems are systems.

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:00.490
And all of the individual
beliefs connect to each other.

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:03.000
So there's many traditional
services organizations

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:05.280
which, for the last
20 or 30 years,

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:08.609
if they were asked what do
they believe about innovation

00:17:08.609 --> 00:17:10.700
and business development,
they would say, well,

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:12.315
it's the job of our staff.

00:17:12.315 --> 00:17:13.980
And because it's
difficult, and slow,

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:15.780
and we're doing
everything ourselves,

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:19.890
we tend to focus on large market
segments to make it economic.

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:23.190
And if we're a bank, we try
to have banking products that

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:24.930
are better than our peers.

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:28.860
And we have to control the
distribution pretty much up

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:30.915
to the line, the point of sale.

00:17:30.915 --> 00:17:32.520
And because we're
doing everything

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:35.040
ourselves and we're
using projects

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.440
to collaborate with
partners, we can handle

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:39.360
a small number of partners.

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:43.680
And two, because we believe our
logo is our brand and drives

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:46.260
recognition of our brand,
we want our customers

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:49.140
to always use our
digital interface.

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:51.690
And we broadcast loads
of marketing messages

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.600
to try and get them to
come to that interface.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:56.510
And this is not a
common belief system

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:59.690
amongst many of the more
modern services companies

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:02.450
that have grown up,
whether they're fintechs

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:04.502
or other types of new
services companies.

00:18:04.502 --> 00:18:05.960
They have an entirely
different set

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:07.850
of beliefs that
all hang together,

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:10.610
that they want their
ecosystem partners to drive

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:15.710
the innovation, and that
they want to be in ecosystems

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:18.050
of collaborating enterprises
that can reach very

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:21.390
small market segments that they
couldn't reach on their own,

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:24.320
and that their brand
experience is driven by how

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:26.510
the brand behaves
inside an ecosystem,

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:28.340
and the crucial skill
for distribution

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:31.040
is orchestrating the
behavior of partners,

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:33.840
and that APIs are fantastic
because they allow you,

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:36.710
when you use the most products,
to manage very large partner

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:39.930
population to drive your growth.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:42.770
And if we think back to
the 1960s and the use

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:45.630
of the development
of the marketing mix,

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:47.540
which had four P's initially--

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:50.060
product, place,
price, and promotion--

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:52.700
these companies believe
that the P for place

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:54.740
is the digital
interface that happens

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:57.290
to be convenient for the
customer at that point in time

00:18:57.290 --> 00:18:59.640
doing what they're doing
in their daily lives.

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:02.540
So in a banking context,
the pay for place

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:05.210
has changed from the
digital interface

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:07.880
that the bank prefers
to the digital interface

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:10.880
that the customer
happens to prefer.

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:14.000
This is a profound
change which gives rise

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.160
to significant changes
in computer systems

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:19.640
and overall belief systems.

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:22.670
And these organizations that
operate with the belief systems

00:19:22.670 --> 00:19:24.920
that are orientated
towards ecosystems,

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:27.590
they're not obsessed with
their brand as a logo.

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:30.770
They're obsessed with getting
the meaning of what they do

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:34.700
and, crucially, why they do it
to communicate it to their end

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:37.760
users so that they can
form an emotional bond

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:39.770
with those clients so
that those clients will

00:19:39.770 --> 00:19:42.620
go looking for those
services as they

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:44.550
go about their daily lives.

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:46.610
So radically different
belief systems.

00:19:46.610 --> 00:19:49.760
And we believe
that the banks that

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:51.440
work on just their
computer systems

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:54.530
and don't consider their
belief systems for ecosystems

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:57.020
will struggle to
scale because they'll

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:00.770
have a highly incoherent
internal environment about how

00:20:00.770 --> 00:20:04.310
exactly they're trying to grow,
and innovate, and partner.

00:20:07.180 --> 00:20:09.730
So this is a simple
graphic we use in Apigee

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:11.450
around the digital value chain.

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:14.710
And this is the old digital
value chain and brand audience

00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:19.060
as we see it, which is
financial brands pushing out

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:21.400
their services
exclusively by and large

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:23.500
through their own
brand up and trying

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:25.390
to both provide
services and form

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:30.240
an emotional brand with a brand
audience made up of customers.

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:32.730
We think this is the
expanded digital value

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:37.380
chain for ecosystems, and it
expands the brand audience

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:40.740
where you're trying to get the
same message about what you

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:43.800
have, but also why you
do it, that message

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:47.610
out to both developers and
customers in this environment.

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:49.920
And that's a crucial thing
because the brand specialist

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:55.200
will tell you that what you do
is important for your services,

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:57.840
but what creates long-term
emotional connections

00:20:57.840 --> 00:21:01.000
to your clients is that they
understand why you do it.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.600
So you need to get that
message out why you do it

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:06.750
and why it's valuable for
society and the economy

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:09.600
and for the customer
out there and have

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:13.350
developers buy in to that too.

00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:15.210
So if you're a
financial brand, these

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:18.750
are the type of brand
messages that are broadcast

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:21.090
and that company
tries to live up

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:24.300
to every day, which is they
are on a long-term journey

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:25.020
with our clients.

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:27.840
They want their clients to
prosper and sleep easily

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:28.380
at night.

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:30.090
They want to protect
them from harm.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:32.160
They want to be the
financial experts.

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:34.680
They want to be available
where their clients need them.

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:37.140
They want to introduce
relevant other services.

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:42.930
These are parts of the
brand purpose of a bank that

00:21:42.930 --> 00:21:45.090
can go back hundreds
of years, and they

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:47.310
will go forward hundreds
of years into the future

00:21:47.310 --> 00:21:49.500
no matter what
technologies evolve.

00:21:49.500 --> 00:21:53.850
But in digital ecosystems, the
same experience of the brand

00:21:53.850 --> 00:21:57.420
needs to be experienced by
third-party developers, who

00:21:57.420 --> 00:22:01.650
should by using the developer
program at the financial brand

00:22:01.650 --> 00:22:03.960
feel that the financial brand
is on a long-term journey

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:07.650
with them, helping them prosper,
helping them sleep at night,

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:10.050
being the financial
experts, being analytical

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:14.070
about the use of APIs, being
structured, protecting them

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:17.190
from harm in terms of
how the APIs are managed,

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.830
being available when they're
needed, and connected

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:21.840
to other wider services.

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:26.610
So same financial purpose in
broad terms of financial brand,

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.950
their job is to look after
financial concerns of people

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:32.430
in the economy and in society,
but digital ecosystems

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:34.220
radically expands
the brand audience.

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:40.090
So financial brands
compete with each other.

00:22:40.090 --> 00:22:42.640
And they need to compete to
become the favorite brands

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:45.070
within the ecosystems,
both for their customers

00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:47.150
and for third-parties.

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:49.000
And we would argue
that the best approach

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.610
inside your traditional
financial brand

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:53.620
is to have the same belief
system for customers

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:54.910
and developers.

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:57.860
Developers are our customers.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:01.280
So it's useful to explore that
topic in a bit more detail.

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:04.240
So in that context,
it's absolutely

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:07.175
natural for bankers today to
go to work in the morning.

00:23:07.175 --> 00:23:09.550
And without setting out their
assumptions or their belief

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:13.810
systems, every task they do
that day is based on this belief

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:16.630
that they want as many
customers at the entry

00:23:16.630 --> 00:23:19.720
level of their service offering
as possible, as many as

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:21.280
possible at entry level.

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:23.620
And they want feedback
from the customers--

00:23:23.620 --> 00:23:25.330
good, bad, and ugly feedback.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:27.820
They want it so
they can improve.

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:30.700
They want to pay attention
particular attention

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:34.600
to the most valuable or
strategic customers, the most

00:23:34.600 --> 00:23:37.780
active ones, the most profitable
ones, the most promising ones.

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:39.820
This is a natural
assumption that underpins

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:41.930
decision making every day.

00:23:41.930 --> 00:23:44.880
They want to assign
suitable staff

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:46.630
to deal with particular
types of customers

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:49.480
with the right qualifications
and skills and attitudes

00:23:49.480 --> 00:23:50.620
and temperaments.

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:52.900
And you want your
customers to be prosperous.

00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:55.712
A financial brand wants
prosperous customers

00:23:55.712 --> 00:23:57.670
because that's the key
to prosperity and profit

00:23:57.670 --> 00:24:00.720
for the financial brand.

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:03.240
You want to onboard
customers quickly and easily.

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:04.830
And you differentiate
the services

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:06.510
that you offer customers.

00:24:06.510 --> 00:24:09.750
And every day,
without specifying

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:11.850
that this is the
underpinning belief,

00:24:11.850 --> 00:24:15.820
banker set about solving
problems for their customers.

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:18.600
We would argue very strongly in
the world of digital ecosystems

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:21.750
that the exact same philosophy
around your brand experience

00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:24.390
should be applied to
third-party developers.

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:27.120
You want as many as
possible at the entry level.

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:29.640
And we find many bankers
and traditional companies

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:31.710
getting hung up on this.

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:34.740
And we try to say,
listen, you understand

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:37.440
it's just like
funneling customers,

00:24:37.440 --> 00:24:39.060
you want as many
possible customers

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:41.340
at the top of the funnel,
and then you filter them.

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:42.875
You want as many
possible developers

00:24:42.875 --> 00:24:45.000
at the top of the funnel,
and then you filter them.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:47.292
And that should be the goal
of your developer marketing

00:24:47.292 --> 00:24:49.080
program.

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:51.090
You want their input.

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:53.400
It's going to be tough for
some traditional companies

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:55.800
when they first begin
their developer program.

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:58.560
And even if they manage
the APIs very well,

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:03.180
they are providing a gateway
into potentially old systems

00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:06.540
or new abstractions of
data from old systems.

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:08.760
You will get some very,
very tough feedback.

00:25:08.760 --> 00:25:11.830
But that feedback loop is going
to be absolutely essential.

00:25:11.830 --> 00:25:14.070
So we see many banks
that are incredibly slow

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:17.010
getting mobilized, and they're
very anxious about the end

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:19.440
state of where is this all
going to end up in terms

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:20.970
of new market structures.

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:22.710
And our encouragement
is, listen,

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:23.940
you have to get started.

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:25.920
You have to create
that feedback loop.

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:27.990
You can't stay here
theorizing about how

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:30.810
this is going to work
for another three years.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:32.160
You need to get started.

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:34.980
You'll get feedback--
good, bad, and ugly.

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:37.500
The bank that gets started
and starts to mobilize

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:40.590
and gets the most feedback
will refine their offering,

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:43.253
will get stronger, and will
form a queue of developers,

00:25:43.253 --> 00:25:45.045
and will start to
generate network effects.

00:25:47.732 --> 00:25:49.190
You want to filter
your developers,

00:25:49.190 --> 00:25:51.570
get the most influential
and profitable ones.

00:25:51.570 --> 00:25:55.730
And in terms of that filtration,
monetization is a tactic.

00:25:55.730 --> 00:25:57.380
We see many banks
getting hung up

00:25:57.380 --> 00:25:59.510
on the idea of API monetization.

00:25:59.510 --> 00:26:01.730
I want to be paid
for all my APIs.

00:26:01.730 --> 00:26:03.470
I want to know who's
going to use them,

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:06.320
how much they're going to
use them, with what volume.

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:08.000
This is not effective.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:11.630
Consider how banks
consider bank fees.

00:26:11.630 --> 00:26:15.050
For certain segments of the
market like startups businesses

00:26:15.050 --> 00:26:19.520
or students, university
students they

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:22.220
don't charge fees because--
or they charge lower fees

00:26:22.220 --> 00:26:25.220
because these are
strategically valuable market

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:27.170
segments in the long term.

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:29.690
Other banking
customers get discounts

00:26:29.690 --> 00:26:31.628
for using a lot of
banking services

00:26:31.628 --> 00:26:33.170
because they're
using them at volume,

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:35.240
so they get a volume discounts.

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:38.450
And other fees are
decided and charged

00:26:38.450 --> 00:26:41.390
based on the nature of
usage, the volume of usage,

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:43.400
the future potential of usage.

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:46.220
And in many cases highly
valuable banking clients

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:49.010
that do a lot of borrowing
or do a lot of depositing

00:26:49.010 --> 00:26:50.870
don't pay any fees.

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:53.510
The exact same principles
apply to API monetization.

00:26:53.510 --> 00:26:54.800
It's a tactic.

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:56.450
If there is a developer
that's driving

00:26:56.450 --> 00:26:58.730
a huge amount of
business traffic

00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:02.930
that allows lots of
deposits to be taken in

00:27:02.930 --> 00:27:05.340
or lots of new
lending to be done,

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:07.610
then you can be quite
relaxed about how much you

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:09.410
charge for the use of the APIs.

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:11.690
You're getting lots
of valuable referrals.

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:15.270
You don't need to charge
that developer for API usage.

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:17.280
So it's just a tactic.

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:20.270
And you learn as you go, and
you refine your pricing tactics

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:22.690
around APIs.

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:25.090
Crucially you want
developers to be prosperous,

00:27:25.090 --> 00:27:27.435
just like you want your
customers to be prosperous.

00:27:27.435 --> 00:27:28.810
And this is
something to focus in

00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:33.190
on because we see some banks
getting very hesitant in case

00:27:33.190 --> 00:27:36.130
third-party developers
combine their banking

00:27:36.130 --> 00:27:40.720
APIs into third-party services
and have a big success.

00:27:40.720 --> 00:27:42.970
And bankers feel
that in some way,

00:27:42.970 --> 00:27:44.620
we've lost out on
the big opportunity

00:27:44.620 --> 00:27:47.602
even though we would never
have had that idea ourselves

00:27:47.602 --> 00:27:49.810
or we would never have
collaborated with that partner

00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:50.920
ourselves.

00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:53.380
And they need to get
over this because there's

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:55.660
no better developer
marketing than having

00:27:55.660 --> 00:27:58.660
one of your developers have
a big success with your APIs.

00:27:58.660 --> 00:28:01.150
You want a big queue of
developers to follow.

00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:04.088
You want as many developers as
possible at the entry level.

00:28:04.088 --> 00:28:06.130
You need to on board and
quickly and easily, just

00:28:06.130 --> 00:28:07.240
like customers.

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:09.490
And just like banks tried
to differentiate our banking

00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:13.930
services, you need to
differentiate your APIs,

00:28:13.930 --> 00:28:16.690
make them faster, make
them more data rich,

00:28:16.690 --> 00:28:19.600
make the documentation better,
make the people to manage

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:22.270
your API program more
progressive, more

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:26.470
knowledgeable, make them more
better relationship managers.

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:29.140
And in this evolution,
because banking

00:28:29.140 --> 00:28:31.150
is thousands of
years old, bankers

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:33.520
will come to the office,
and the only difference

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:37.250
will be is that they want
to solve problems of both

00:28:37.250 --> 00:28:40.330
their customers and third-party
developers with the same energy

00:28:40.330 --> 00:28:44.560
and the same purpose, because
they'll be part of their brand

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:47.740
audience, and they will
serve their brand purpose

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:49.900
and will drive new
growth and new innovation

00:28:49.900 --> 00:28:52.060
and partnering for banks
and financial services

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:54.370
companies in digital ecosystems.

00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:00.500
So they're strong
conclusions that Apigee

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:06.290
has derived from its
fantastic vantage point

00:29:06.290 --> 00:29:08.540
in the marketplace
across all verticals.

00:29:08.540 --> 00:29:11.540
And there they are
again delighted to deal

00:29:11.540 --> 00:29:15.830
in our fireside chat, if people
have questions or ideas related

00:29:15.830 --> 00:29:18.290
to this.

00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:18.790
OK.

00:29:21.610 --> 00:29:25.840
All right, so now let's
pause and get some questions

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:28.000
from the audience.

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:32.800
And once more, to ask questions,
please use the link here

00:29:32.800 --> 00:29:36.025
or click on the Ask Question
button on your Watch page.

00:29:38.670 --> 00:29:42.630
KIKI MARCONDES: So the
first question here.

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:47.960
Some people say ecosystems will
turn banks into dumb pipes.

00:29:47.960 --> 00:29:49.850
What do you think
about that, Paul?

00:29:49.850 --> 00:29:53.150
PAUL ROHAN: At Apigee, we
get this question a lot.

00:29:53.150 --> 00:29:55.640
And sometimes we have
turn on pickets of,

00:29:55.640 --> 00:29:57.350
what you mean by dumb pipes.

00:29:57.350 --> 00:30:01.490
But in many ways, we see
it as a cultural reaction

00:30:01.490 --> 00:30:05.750
to new customer journeys which
other companies are generating

00:30:05.750 --> 00:30:08.510
and a feeling that if
you're not in full control

00:30:08.510 --> 00:30:12.060
of the entire journey yourself
with your logo on the front,

00:30:12.060 --> 00:30:14.480
that something
major has been lost.

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:17.690
And all we can do is step
back from that and ask--

00:30:17.690 --> 00:30:21.650
well, forget about pipes, what
might be dumb going forward,

00:30:21.650 --> 00:30:22.400
OK?

00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:24.410
And coming back to
the age of assistance

00:30:24.410 --> 00:30:27.230
where young people
are increasingly

00:30:27.230 --> 00:30:29.750
used to these connected journeys
with their favorite brands

00:30:29.750 --> 00:30:33.110
collaborating and providing
a seamless experience

00:30:33.110 --> 00:30:35.150
where they might end up
buying several things

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:38.720
from several companies, but it's
been one connected experience,

00:30:38.720 --> 00:30:42.410
you have to ask yourself is
it dumb to broadcast messages

00:30:42.410 --> 00:30:44.840
to those young people,
those young businesses that

00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:47.960
are becoming increasingly
enmeshed in this world,

00:30:47.960 --> 00:30:49.910
saying come and look
at our proprietary app,

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:53.240
come and look, break the chain,
break your concentration,

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:57.020
break the convenience, and come
and read up on what we have,

00:30:57.020 --> 00:30:59.000
and you'll probably find
it marginally better

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:02.450
as a core product than
something you've just bought.

00:31:02.450 --> 00:31:04.310
Too late, OK?

00:31:04.310 --> 00:31:07.520
So in the context of that,
we would say step back

00:31:07.520 --> 00:31:09.680
because it's a very
emotional statement,

00:31:09.680 --> 00:31:13.640
and ask what in this-- is an
environmental change coming?

00:31:13.640 --> 00:31:17.570
But financial services are
still very valuable and rare.

00:31:17.570 --> 00:31:20.450
It's very hard to get a license,
particularly to take deposits.

00:31:20.450 --> 00:31:23.130
Highly regulated, you
need a lot of capital.

00:31:23.130 --> 00:31:25.850
This is not a business
that many organizations

00:31:25.850 --> 00:31:27.270
will get into easily.

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:29.930
So in that context,
we asked banks

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:33.140
to separate the core purpose
of what they do as bankers

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:36.320
and how all services
are changing.

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:38.280
And once they step
back from that,

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:41.090
we find the question of
dumb pipe, dumb pipe,

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:43.610
we'll be a dumb pipe
is far less sinister.

00:31:43.610 --> 00:31:45.800
And then they start to
see the upside, which

00:31:45.800 --> 00:31:49.340
is, if I'm not fully in control
of the customer journey,

00:31:49.340 --> 00:31:52.170
yes, perhaps my logo
is not on every screen.

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:55.220
But if I use API products, I can
be in 10 times as many customer

00:31:55.220 --> 00:31:56.280
journeys.

00:31:56.280 --> 00:32:01.580
And I can have a realistic
aspiration to double my sales,

00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:03.992
while I'm in 10 times as
many customer journeys.

00:32:03.992 --> 00:32:05.450
And if you have
doubled your sales,

00:32:05.450 --> 00:32:07.492
that's going to pay for
a lot of marketing budget

00:32:07.492 --> 00:32:09.620
to help get across
your brand message, OK.

00:32:09.620 --> 00:32:11.390
So we ask people
to step through it.

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:15.980
But it is a question, and it
does reflect this belief system

00:32:15.980 --> 00:32:19.130
that we think is
very established,

00:32:19.130 --> 00:32:22.430
but increasingly might be the
wrong belief system to have

00:32:22.430 --> 00:32:25.898
about how bankers should
distribute their services.

00:32:25.898 --> 00:32:27.190
KIKI MARCONDES: That was great.

00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:28.580
Thank you so much.

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:31.180
Thanks so much from that.

00:32:31.180 --> 00:32:33.250
Thanks for the questions.

00:32:33.250 --> 00:32:36.550
We're going to start
the fireside chat now.

00:32:36.550 --> 00:32:38.320
And I just would
like to remind you

00:32:38.320 --> 00:32:42.190
that we will keep
answering questions

00:32:42.190 --> 00:32:46.540
during the fireside chat, so
please keep them coming, over

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:47.410
to you, Ollie.

00:32:47.410 --> 00:32:48.160
OLIVER OGG: Great.

00:32:48.160 --> 00:32:49.870
Thanks, Kiki.

00:32:49.870 --> 00:32:52.390
Paul, a fantastic session.

00:32:52.390 --> 00:32:56.080
You've got this unique
global perspective, often

00:32:56.080 --> 00:32:58.630
traveling around the world.

00:32:58.630 --> 00:33:00.940
When you look across the
entire landscape, I mean,

00:33:00.940 --> 00:33:03.940
who's kind of leading the way?

00:33:03.940 --> 00:33:06.475
PAUL ROHAN: We see a lot
of leaders and laggards.

00:33:06.475 --> 00:33:10.600
But it's difficult to
define an absolute leader

00:33:10.600 --> 00:33:15.280
based on geography,
OK, because there's

00:33:15.280 --> 00:33:16.790
different environmental factors.

00:33:16.790 --> 00:33:18.700
So there's developing
economies, for example,

00:33:18.700 --> 00:33:22.450
where there's never
been home broadband,

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:24.580
there's never been
a home laptops.

00:33:24.580 --> 00:33:26.268
And there's a whole
new generation

00:33:26.268 --> 00:33:28.060
of people getting
connected to the internet

00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:30.700
for the first time.

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:32.050
It's all mobile.

00:33:32.050 --> 00:33:33.550
It's all cellular.

00:33:33.550 --> 00:33:37.803
And to give context, 10 years
ago one third of the world's

00:33:37.803 --> 00:33:39.970
global middle classes who
are heavy users of banking

00:33:39.970 --> 00:33:43.390
services from financial
brands was in Asia.

00:33:43.390 --> 00:33:46.240
In 10 more years, so
in a 20 year shift,

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:49.570
2/3 of the global middle
classes would be in Asia.

00:33:49.570 --> 00:33:52.252
And those new consumers
of banking services

00:33:52.252 --> 00:33:53.710
will never have
had home broadband,

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:54.835
will never had home laptop.

00:33:54.835 --> 00:33:56.560
It'll all be mobile.

00:33:56.560 --> 00:33:57.880
It'll all be cellular.

00:33:57.880 --> 00:34:02.440
And in that context, and
there is banks in Asia

00:34:02.440 --> 00:34:05.078
who are only building APIs
and not building apps,

00:34:05.078 --> 00:34:07.120
because they don't think
apps are going to scale.

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:08.260
There's going to be
hundreds of millions

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:11.050
of people who are new customers
with different languages

00:34:11.050 --> 00:34:13.193
that will be part of
the platform economy.

00:34:13.193 --> 00:34:14.860
They might be managing
their daily lives

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:18.340
to a Chinese platform or to an
American platform or another--

00:34:18.340 --> 00:34:19.630
an Indian platform.

00:34:19.630 --> 00:34:22.120
They need to expose
banking APIs.

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:25.210
So they're very
advanced with APIs,

00:34:25.210 --> 00:34:29.739
but they're driven
by their environment.

00:34:29.739 --> 00:34:31.719
Regions where we
see leaders that

00:34:31.719 --> 00:34:34.000
are more progressive
as institutions,

00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:36.460
we think it comes down
to executive leadership,

00:34:36.460 --> 00:34:40.670
OK, because things like
talking about a new belief

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:43.280
system and a new brand
strategy for ecosystem,

00:34:43.280 --> 00:34:45.380
that's a C-suite job.

00:34:45.380 --> 00:34:49.760
And when you're trying to
use new techniques like APIs

00:34:49.760 --> 00:34:52.730
and new market segments
with new partners,

00:34:52.730 --> 00:34:54.770
people get a lot more
nervous about things

00:34:54.770 --> 00:34:58.040
going wrong than their
tried and tested methods

00:34:58.040 --> 00:35:02.000
that they've used every
day for years and everyone

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:05.060
knows what to expect and most
risk management techniques have

00:35:05.060 --> 00:35:09.260
been worked out, so
a lot less nervous.

00:35:09.260 --> 00:35:13.630
And when there's a setback
in a very mature environment,

00:35:13.630 --> 00:35:15.520
people get over it
faster and move on.

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:17.830
Whereas, a setback in
this brand new world

00:35:17.830 --> 00:35:21.400
where people are uncertain feel
like the sky is falling in, OK.

00:35:21.400 --> 00:35:24.220
So as people are
making the transition,

00:35:24.220 --> 00:35:27.850
we think into this new mindset
and a new belief system,

00:35:27.850 --> 00:35:31.460
not only do the C-suite need
to fire the starting gun,

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:35.690
but they need to stay engaged,
OK, because they're learning,

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:36.730
people are learning.

00:35:36.730 --> 00:35:39.400
And you need to the
C-suite to constantly keep

00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:41.420
that strategic goal in mind.

00:35:41.420 --> 00:35:42.160
It's a setback.

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:42.920
It's a mistake.

00:35:42.920 --> 00:35:45.100
Let's fix it, move
on, move on, move on.

00:35:45.100 --> 00:35:48.160
We're entering into a new,
unfamiliar, strange market.

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:51.050
It's like landing in
a foreign country.

00:35:51.050 --> 00:35:52.120
We don't know the people.

00:35:52.120 --> 00:35:53.453
We don't know the best segments.

00:35:53.453 --> 00:35:55.282
We don't know the
best products to sell.

00:35:55.282 --> 00:35:57.740
We got to get around the place
and figure out how it works.

00:35:57.740 --> 00:35:59.865
And then we'll set very
tough targets for ourselves

00:35:59.865 --> 00:36:01.000
and then we'll scale.

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:04.300
So we deal with a
lot of organizations.

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:08.530
And the ones that are least
tentative and most progressive

00:36:08.530 --> 00:36:11.140
and get over the learning
experience faster

00:36:11.140 --> 00:36:14.560
are the ones where
the C-suite called out

00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:17.740
the culture changes as well
as the technology changes.

00:36:17.740 --> 00:36:20.562
And then don't just treat it
as another project, come back

00:36:20.562 --> 00:36:22.270
to us in six months
when you're finished,

00:36:22.270 --> 00:36:25.090
because the odds
of being success

00:36:25.090 --> 00:36:27.580
in a very established
environment

00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:30.470
is radically reduced, if
they don't stay engaged.

00:36:30.470 --> 00:36:35.380
So leadership comes
from leadership.

00:36:35.380 --> 00:36:38.080
OLIVER OGG: And
how much permission

00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:41.590
does a bank have to get from
that, say, their regulator, is

00:36:41.590 --> 00:36:44.483
that an excuse to execute?

00:36:44.483 --> 00:36:46.150
Because there must
be some regions where

00:36:46.150 --> 00:36:50.110
I guess the local regulator has
yet to say, you must do this.

00:36:50.110 --> 00:36:51.100
PAUL ROHAN: Yeah.

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:54.220
The regulatory
environments do differ,

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:59.210
but bankers can get frustrated,
very frustrated sometimes

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:01.310
with what the
regulators specify.

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:04.350
And some of that's justified
and some of it's not.

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:06.290
But you have to
ask ourselves, why

00:37:06.290 --> 00:37:09.740
are regulators doing this
or even considering this?

00:37:09.740 --> 00:37:13.460
Because they issued licenses
to people to run banks

00:37:13.460 --> 00:37:16.220
that will finance the
growth of the economy.

00:37:16.220 --> 00:37:19.730
So regulators are looking at
how the economy is evolving.

00:37:19.730 --> 00:37:22.220
And it's the age of assistance.

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:24.410
Companies are connecting
to each other.

00:37:24.410 --> 00:37:28.940
Consumer experiences are highly
digital and highly connected.

00:37:28.940 --> 00:37:32.150
And regulators are
setting a policy framework

00:37:32.150 --> 00:37:38.030
around consent and privacy and
all of those conduct rules.

00:37:38.030 --> 00:37:40.730
But they are there
underpin good practice

00:37:40.730 --> 00:37:43.370
in a world that's
getting connected.

00:37:43.370 --> 00:37:47.000
So we encourage our
clients to step--

00:37:47.000 --> 00:37:50.510
yes, Article 58 might be
ambiguous and frustrating,

00:37:50.510 --> 00:37:53.060
and if only the regulators
around the world

00:37:53.060 --> 00:37:55.940
would get together and
synchronize everything.

00:37:55.940 --> 00:37:58.130
Put that aside and
ask yourselves,

00:37:58.130 --> 00:37:59.900
why are they doing this?

00:37:59.900 --> 00:38:00.900
Why are they doing this?

00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:04.927
And not only is it the age
of assistance and banks,

00:38:04.927 --> 00:38:07.010
if they want to stay
connected to their customers,

00:38:07.010 --> 00:38:08.420
need to connect.

00:38:08.420 --> 00:38:12.020
But regulators with whatever
degree of effectiveness

00:38:12.020 --> 00:38:15.050
are setting themselves
up to regulate conduct

00:38:15.050 --> 00:38:16.520
in the age of assistance.

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:18.290
So step up to the why.

00:38:18.290 --> 00:38:21.290
And we think that some
banks that get obsessed

00:38:21.290 --> 00:38:24.710
about the regulatory
micro decisions

00:38:24.710 --> 00:38:26.880
are at risk of missing
the big picture.

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:28.910
Because then they send a
team over in the corner

00:38:28.910 --> 00:38:30.335
to deal with the regulations.

00:38:30.335 --> 00:38:32.960
And what they should be doing is
getting the whole organization

00:38:32.960 --> 00:38:36.590
to consider, how do we develop
and persist with our brand

00:38:36.590 --> 00:38:39.980
in this new way of services
being designed and delivered

00:38:39.980 --> 00:38:40.680
and consumed?

00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:41.430
OLIVER OGG: Great.

00:38:41.430 --> 00:38:43.160
Great.

00:38:43.160 --> 00:38:47.810
So I guess, I see a common
kind of dichotomy of execution

00:38:47.810 --> 00:38:52.700
between those that just see it
in banking as a regulatory tax

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:55.280
to those that are desperately
trying to engage with fintechs,

00:38:55.280 --> 00:38:59.060
perhaps desperately trying
to speed up viable ventures

00:38:59.060 --> 00:39:01.550
within larger groups.

00:39:01.550 --> 00:39:04.160
There's a lot of-- so If
we shift the conversation

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:06.710
from regulatory to the
innovation spectrum,

00:39:06.710 --> 00:39:08.660
there's a lot of
innovation theater going on

00:39:08.660 --> 00:39:09.560
in the industry.

00:39:09.560 --> 00:39:12.680
What are some of the kind of
common pitfalls that you see?

00:39:17.230 --> 00:39:20.350
PAUL ROHAN: I think most of
our cultural in that we've--

00:39:20.350 --> 00:39:23.530
I think it's a step
forward and a step

00:39:23.530 --> 00:39:25.750
back to create an innovation
department in the corner

00:39:25.750 --> 00:39:27.208
where everyone
takes their ties off

00:39:27.208 --> 00:39:28.960
and you have bean
bags instead of desks.

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:31.180
It's a step forward
in that we need

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:33.910
to loosen up and think about
doing things in new ways

00:39:33.910 --> 00:39:36.640
and with new partners
and in new segments.

00:39:36.640 --> 00:39:39.535
But at the same time too
with the wrong leadership

00:39:39.535 --> 00:39:41.410
or insufficient leadership,
it becomes, well,

00:39:41.410 --> 00:39:46.060
that's their job, and
incremental minor changes

00:39:46.060 --> 00:39:47.600
is business as usual.

00:39:47.600 --> 00:39:51.980
So in the context
of that, there's

00:39:51.980 --> 00:39:54.650
a socialization challenge
right across the organization.

00:39:54.650 --> 00:39:57.933
And I feel for some
traditional banks,

00:39:57.933 --> 00:40:00.350
sometimes for fun, we say we
think they're making progress

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:03.500
because we see the
suits, OK, with the ties

00:40:03.500 --> 00:40:07.400
on, but actually talking
in detail about the culture

00:40:07.400 --> 00:40:09.102
of the ecosystem
and how do I grow

00:40:09.102 --> 00:40:10.685
and, yes, I need to
do the governance,

00:40:10.685 --> 00:40:12.530
and yes, I need to do
the risk management.

00:40:12.530 --> 00:40:14.640
But how much is enough?

00:40:14.640 --> 00:40:15.860
How much is too much?

00:40:15.860 --> 00:40:19.110
And so therefore, you
have to ask yourself,

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:21.650
what does the ecosystem
want from banks?

00:40:21.650 --> 00:40:25.370
They want stuff that
nonbanks can't do.

00:40:25.370 --> 00:40:28.070
They want stuff that you
can't do without a license.

00:40:28.070 --> 00:40:29.690
All of that stuff
that is regulated

00:40:29.690 --> 00:40:32.300
because badly managed
banks blow up economies,

00:40:32.300 --> 00:40:34.640
so therefore they get regulated.

00:40:34.640 --> 00:40:37.610
So once you start to say,
well, what do we have

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:40.160
that's valuable
and rare as a bank

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:43.490
that's hard for a nonbanks
to imitate or substitute,

00:40:43.490 --> 00:40:44.510
that's the card.

00:40:44.510 --> 00:40:46.640
It's payments at high scale.

00:40:46.640 --> 00:40:47.723
It's loans at high scale.

00:40:47.723 --> 00:40:48.890
It's deposits at high scale.

00:40:48.890 --> 00:40:50.610
It's investments at high scale.

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:52.495
And that's the old systems.

00:40:52.495 --> 00:40:54.120
You need to get the
data obstructer out

00:40:54.120 --> 00:40:57.050
of those systems,
turn it into APIs,

00:40:57.050 --> 00:40:59.600
start to learn the feedback
from the developers.

00:40:59.600 --> 00:41:01.490
So there is no shortcut.

00:41:01.490 --> 00:41:04.040
And the risk is that
innovation theater, which

00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:07.520
can be superficial, delays
the real innovation, which

00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:09.980
is what do we do that
no one else can do,

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:11.558
because the ecosystem wants it?

00:41:11.558 --> 00:41:13.850
OLIVER OGG: And this kind of
ties into my next question

00:41:13.850 --> 00:41:16.430
and something you just
referred to earlier

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:19.370
around failure and learning.

00:41:19.370 --> 00:41:21.740
When you're working
with C-suite,

00:41:21.740 --> 00:41:24.150
do you talk about that in
perhaps a bit more detail,

00:41:24.150 --> 00:41:27.740
because I think I see
others sort of pronounce it

00:41:27.740 --> 00:41:29.690
as if this is OK to do.

00:41:29.690 --> 00:41:32.870
But actually I think
when it came to it,

00:41:32.870 --> 00:41:37.070
failure would not be accepted
and heads would be removed.

00:41:37.070 --> 00:41:38.510
PAUL ROHAN: I
think you have to--

00:41:38.510 --> 00:41:41.250
I think you have to connect
failure to projects culture,

00:41:41.250 --> 00:41:41.930
OK.

00:41:41.930 --> 00:41:43.790
So why does a
traditional organization

00:41:43.790 --> 00:41:45.225
fear failure so much?

00:41:45.225 --> 00:41:46.475
Because projects are terrible.

00:41:49.247 --> 00:41:50.830
Compared to business
as usual, they're

00:41:50.830 --> 00:41:52.663
incredibly difficult to execute.

00:41:52.663 --> 00:41:54.830
They are temporary and
unique management structures,

00:41:54.830 --> 00:41:56.997
full of people who are
reporting to different people

00:41:56.997 --> 00:41:58.550
on temporary assignment.

00:41:58.550 --> 00:42:01.070
Most of them don't
reach their goals.

00:42:01.070 --> 00:42:03.440
And inevitably, the vast
amount of work they do

00:42:03.440 --> 00:42:04.670
is not reusable.

00:42:04.670 --> 00:42:06.830
So when you're trying
to innovate and partner

00:42:06.830 --> 00:42:10.460
particularly through projects,
failure is a horrible prospect.

00:42:10.460 --> 00:42:12.140
A, It's likely.

00:42:12.140 --> 00:42:14.610
And B, it's really expensive.

00:42:14.610 --> 00:42:17.000
So I don't think you can just
say failure and isolation.

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:20.180
I think fear of failure comes
from feeling that there's

00:42:20.180 --> 00:42:23.210
no alternative to projects
and they're an expensive way

00:42:23.210 --> 00:42:26.390
to fail for all of those
reasons we just said.

00:42:26.390 --> 00:42:27.950
So in that context
when you're trying

00:42:27.950 --> 00:42:30.830
to say there's some API
products, if you systematically

00:42:30.830 --> 00:42:33.830
start to build them and use
them and market them and get

00:42:33.830 --> 00:42:38.010
feedback on them, your failures
are far more economic, so

00:42:38.010 --> 00:42:39.920
far faster.

00:42:39.920 --> 00:42:42.530
The stuff you did to reach
the failure far faster

00:42:42.530 --> 00:42:44.840
is reusable or a lot
of it's reusable.

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:48.080
And you're actively
marketing the outcome

00:42:48.080 --> 00:42:51.950
from your innovation to not
just the first person to use it,

00:42:51.950 --> 00:42:54.090
but to the next hundred
who could use it.

00:42:54.090 --> 00:42:57.797
So I think when
people say, listen,

00:42:57.797 --> 00:42:59.630
everyone is afraid of
failure here, it's no.

00:42:59.630 --> 00:43:01.610
It's the people are
afraid of failure

00:43:01.610 --> 00:43:05.600
because they think there's no
scalable valuable alternatives

00:43:05.600 --> 00:43:07.130
to projects culture.

00:43:07.130 --> 00:43:07.880
There is.

00:43:07.880 --> 00:43:09.860
API products culture, OK.

00:43:09.860 --> 00:43:11.600
And I think once
that feeling starts

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:15.350
to-- once an understanding
starts to come and then

00:43:15.350 --> 00:43:17.330
crucially support
it from the top,

00:43:17.330 --> 00:43:21.260
then the idea of
partnering is API enabled.

00:43:21.260 --> 00:43:23.360
It's reusable investment.

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:25.160
I can exit quickly if
the partner turns out

00:43:25.160 --> 00:43:27.380
to disappoint, they
don't produce the volume.

00:43:27.380 --> 00:43:28.242
It's reusable.

00:43:28.242 --> 00:43:29.450
There's automation behind it.

00:43:29.450 --> 00:43:31.170
I have a marketing program.

00:43:31.170 --> 00:43:34.400
It's as much-- it's an entire
new belief system around how

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:38.380
you're going to collaborate then
projects and oh, it's awful,

00:43:38.380 --> 00:43:38.880
I hate it.

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:40.047
How do I get off this thing?

00:43:40.047 --> 00:43:41.280
OLIVER OGG: Yeah, great.

00:43:41.280 --> 00:43:43.280
I had a moment of realization
actually this year

00:43:43.280 --> 00:43:50.030
when I was in a form of maybe
an 18 C-suite of Mia insurers,

00:43:50.030 --> 00:43:51.680
mainly CIOs.

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:54.530
And the topic was ecosystems.

00:43:54.530 --> 00:43:57.740
But I very rapidly realized
actually that quite a few

00:43:57.740 --> 00:44:01.760
had come to that
session with a mindset

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:05.270
that an ecosystem included
strategic alliances.

00:44:05.270 --> 00:44:08.150
So I had to really
bring that room along

00:44:08.150 --> 00:44:09.720
into sort of saying,
well, actually

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:11.540
that this is our perspective.

00:44:11.540 --> 00:44:13.790
Is that something that you
see in the world of banking

00:44:13.790 --> 00:44:14.290
as well.

00:44:14.290 --> 00:44:17.665
Because I guess banks have been
at the core of an ecosystem.

00:44:17.665 --> 00:44:19.040
I think of the
Medici's here, you

00:44:19.040 --> 00:44:22.112
know, very vibrant ecosystems
for hundreds of years.

00:44:22.112 --> 00:44:22.820
PAUL ROHAN: Yeah.

00:44:22.820 --> 00:44:25.880
Sorry, I'm not saying that
strategic alliances will never

00:44:25.880 --> 00:44:26.670
be used.

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:30.207
But if you want to have 1,000
partners in an ecosystem

00:44:30.207 --> 00:44:31.790
driving your growth,
you want to do it

00:44:31.790 --> 00:44:33.110
through strategic alliances.

00:44:33.110 --> 00:44:35.750
It has to be API
enabled partnering.

00:44:35.750 --> 00:44:39.860
And I do think it's a key
part of the cultural shift.

00:44:39.860 --> 00:44:41.750
So you have to say
to people sometimes,

00:44:41.750 --> 00:44:44.247
listen, you're talking
to fintechs at the moment

00:44:44.247 --> 00:44:45.830
are you looking at
potential partners,

00:44:45.830 --> 00:44:49.250
and you imagine who it might
be and you organize a lunch.

00:44:49.250 --> 00:44:51.925
You talk about how
you might collaborate.

00:44:51.925 --> 00:44:53.300
And then you
assemble projects in

00:44:53.300 --> 00:44:54.800
your respective organization.

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:59.600
Even this description
of it is slow.

00:44:59.600 --> 00:45:03.410
But consider what you define
by a partnering opportunity.

00:45:03.410 --> 00:45:06.650
Should it be when somebody
who you've never met

00:45:06.650 --> 00:45:08.600
has started consuming
your APIs and not

00:45:08.600 --> 00:45:10.640
just consuming it,
doing it in ways

00:45:10.640 --> 00:45:12.320
that you would never
have thought about,

00:45:12.320 --> 00:45:14.240
is bringing your services
into new segments

00:45:14.240 --> 00:45:15.950
that you never
reached on your own?

00:45:15.950 --> 00:45:17.960
They're paying
for the privilege.

00:45:17.960 --> 00:45:19.580
Now it's the time
to have a lunch

00:45:19.580 --> 00:45:22.010
and say, should this be
a strategic Alliance.

00:45:22.010 --> 00:45:25.310
So coming back to the idea
of treating developers

00:45:25.310 --> 00:45:28.760
as customers, you want
as many possible partners

00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:32.030
at the entry level, and then
begin the filtration process

00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:34.850
through API monetization.

00:45:34.850 --> 00:45:37.070
So if you think about
fintechs and banks

00:45:37.070 --> 00:45:38.845
were talking to a
lot of fintechs,

00:45:38.845 --> 00:45:40.970
and there's been a lot of
lunches and lots of beers

00:45:40.970 --> 00:45:44.120
and lots of coffees, the
question should be, now

00:45:44.120 --> 00:45:46.910
can you use APIs to
filter those fintechs that

00:45:46.910 --> 00:45:50.600
want to talk to your bank
into those that can just talk

00:45:50.600 --> 00:45:53.810
and those who can talk and
actually simultaneously drive

00:45:53.810 --> 00:45:56.090
traffic into your
organization through your APIs

00:45:56.090 --> 00:45:57.280
and pay for it?

00:45:57.280 --> 00:45:58.030
OLIVER OGG: Great.

00:45:58.030 --> 00:46:00.080
And I see there's
a question that's

00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:01.580
come through that's
somewhat related

00:46:01.580 --> 00:46:02.800
to the theme of ecosystem.

00:46:02.800 --> 00:46:05.210
So let's ask this one.

00:46:05.210 --> 00:46:08.210
Does Google-- or I'll
just rephrase it slightly.

00:46:08.210 --> 00:46:12.380
Has Google created a marketplace
for financial services or will

00:46:12.380 --> 00:46:14.442
you do?

00:46:14.442 --> 00:46:17.780
PAUL ROHAN: It's a very
beguiling title, marketplace

00:46:17.780 --> 00:46:19.190
for financial services.

00:46:19.190 --> 00:46:22.820
And financial services is
an incredibly broad church

00:46:22.820 --> 00:46:25.970
in terms of what it offers
from the most sophisticated

00:46:25.970 --> 00:46:28.400
investment clients down
to the simplest startup

00:46:28.400 --> 00:46:30.980
or junior savings account.

00:46:30.980 --> 00:46:33.840
And Google is a
global organization.

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:37.850
So in the context of, has
Google created a marketplace

00:46:37.850 --> 00:46:40.160
for financial services,
it's hard to describe

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:42.230
what that might be, OK.

00:46:42.230 --> 00:46:46.130
There's no doubt that if
you step back and consider

00:46:46.130 --> 00:46:51.200
ecosystems as a place where
there are connected experiences

00:46:51.200 --> 00:46:55.190
across their favorite brands,
Google is a very popular brand.

00:46:55.190 --> 00:46:57.440
People connect
through Google Pay.

00:46:57.440 --> 00:46:58.820
Banks connect to Google Maps.

00:46:58.820 --> 00:47:02.390
They connect to all sorts
of Google digital assets.

00:47:02.390 --> 00:47:05.180
That type of, you
know, are you going

00:47:05.180 --> 00:47:08.330
to create something
monolithic where you can see?

00:47:08.330 --> 00:47:10.910
It's a connected economy
in the age of assistance.

00:47:10.910 --> 00:47:14.000
Google has vast numbers
of APIs, vast numbers

00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:15.950
of collaborating partners.

00:47:15.950 --> 00:47:18.590
Step back from
thinking that there's

00:47:18.590 --> 00:47:21.590
going to be huge moves by
horizontal organizations

00:47:21.590 --> 00:47:23.600
or vertical organizations.

00:47:23.600 --> 00:47:25.280
People should
consider what will be

00:47:25.280 --> 00:47:28.790
the design of the overall
marketplace for all systems

00:47:28.790 --> 00:47:30.510
and for all services.

00:47:30.510 --> 00:47:33.200
So you're going to find
insurance companies are going

00:47:33.200 --> 00:47:36.920
to be dabbling in segments that
they would never have touched

00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:38.960
when they had their
old business model,

00:47:38.960 --> 00:47:43.430
but now will make some money out
of stuff that's very partially

00:47:43.430 --> 00:47:44.780
related to insurance.

00:47:44.780 --> 00:47:46.220
Banks will start
to make money out

00:47:46.220 --> 00:47:49.950
of nonbanking activities
and ecosystems.

00:47:49.950 --> 00:47:53.450
So we think that
developing strategy based

00:47:53.450 --> 00:47:56.960
on the positioning
of other companies

00:47:56.960 --> 00:47:58.940
is increasingly impossible.

00:47:58.940 --> 00:48:03.110
So 10 years ago you could do a
strategy session and everyone

00:48:03.110 --> 00:48:05.450
does the SWOT
analysis on the wall--

00:48:05.450 --> 00:48:08.000
strengths, weaknesses,
opportunities, and threats.

00:48:08.000 --> 00:48:10.460
And when fintechs
came along, bankers

00:48:10.460 --> 00:48:13.460
would write collaboration
with 100 fintech names

00:48:13.460 --> 00:48:14.810
down as an opportunity.

00:48:14.810 --> 00:48:17.450
And then they'd post the
same 100 fintech names down

00:48:17.450 --> 00:48:19.250
as a threat.

00:48:19.250 --> 00:48:23.120
And all that said was, you can't
devise a strategy that everyone

00:48:23.120 --> 00:48:24.620
in the company
understands and is

00:48:24.620 --> 00:48:31.520
pursuing based on what are those
47 potential rivals doing now.

00:48:31.520 --> 00:48:34.310
If there's a big shift in
the design of the markets

00:48:34.310 --> 00:48:36.740
and how it operates, you have
to shift your positioning

00:48:36.740 --> 00:48:38.540
towards that design.

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:43.250
So analysts have described
strategy of it's a plan

00:48:43.250 --> 00:48:46.280
and it's a position and
it's a pattern of activity

00:48:46.280 --> 00:48:48.770
and all different
ways to look at it.

00:48:48.770 --> 00:48:50.390
And we think the
pattern of activity

00:48:50.390 --> 00:48:53.510
is changing radically, follow
the pattern of activity

00:48:53.510 --> 00:48:55.490
in terms of how you behave.

00:48:55.490 --> 00:48:59.390
Trying to figure out what 100
other companies are going to do

00:48:59.390 --> 00:49:02.720
might be an interesting
discussion to have over coffee.

00:49:02.720 --> 00:49:05.000
However, it doesn't
produce anything sensible

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:08.540
for your organization to figure
out in terms of its priorities

00:49:08.540 --> 00:49:10.590
for the age of assistance.

00:49:10.590 --> 00:49:11.520
OLIVER OGG: Great.

00:49:11.520 --> 00:49:17.460
So let's say tomorrow you go
home, and on your doorstep

00:49:17.460 --> 00:49:20.970
is a letter from a bank
with a very attractive offer

00:49:20.970 --> 00:49:23.430
and you want to become the CIO.

00:49:23.430 --> 00:49:25.560
You've had enough
of the Google kale,

00:49:25.560 --> 00:49:28.692
the random inflatable
dinosaurs, whatever.

00:49:28.692 --> 00:49:30.150
PAUL ROHAN: No more
green trousers.

00:49:30.150 --> 00:49:33.030
OLIVER OGG: No
more green chinos.

00:49:33.030 --> 00:49:35.730
Now you're looking at this
future untapped technology

00:49:35.730 --> 00:49:40.650
landscape of AI,
ML, API, blockchain.

00:49:40.650 --> 00:49:43.260
What's the right
level of focus the CIO

00:49:43.260 --> 00:49:49.860
should have on this
feature landscape for APIs?

00:49:49.860 --> 00:49:51.300
PAUL ROHAN: I
think it was Thomas

00:49:51.300 --> 00:49:56.010
Edison said that genius
is 1% inspiration 99%

00:49:56.010 --> 00:49:57.660
is perspiration.

00:49:57.660 --> 00:50:01.260
So for a traditional
bank, the CIO

00:50:01.260 --> 00:50:04.680
getting the valuable services
out of the core legacy systems

00:50:04.680 --> 00:50:08.340
and into an API
layer, there's got

00:50:08.340 --> 00:50:11.820
to be a lot of perspiration,
OK, and there's no shortcuts.

00:50:11.820 --> 00:50:16.290
But as that's happening,
the 1% of the time

00:50:16.290 --> 00:50:21.300
that's the most valuable is
evangelizing for the change

00:50:21.300 --> 00:50:22.680
in market dynamics.

00:50:22.680 --> 00:50:25.200
And in some of those
topics we've just

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:28.278
discovered looking for
inspiration in what's

00:50:28.278 --> 00:50:29.820
happening in other
service industries

00:50:29.820 --> 00:50:33.480
in terms of how they're
using APIs and ecosystems

00:50:33.480 --> 00:50:36.743
and focusing on developers
as a new brand audience

00:50:36.743 --> 00:50:38.910
to make sure that they come
to have the same respect

00:50:38.910 --> 00:50:42.510
and affinity to your
bank as your customers,

00:50:42.510 --> 00:50:46.560
but arguably say very
strongly to banker colleagues,

00:50:46.560 --> 00:50:49.530
really the purpose of
a bank hasn't changed.

00:50:49.530 --> 00:50:52.388
How services are designed
and developed and delivered

00:50:52.388 --> 00:50:54.180
are changing across
all service industries,

00:50:54.180 --> 00:50:56.880
but financial
services remains quite

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:01.350
a small, regulated,
highly-capitalized, tough

00:51:01.350 --> 00:51:02.580
business.

00:51:02.580 --> 00:51:05.100
It's not every business
is going to get into it.

00:51:05.100 --> 00:51:08.550
We just need to
rethink the service

00:51:08.550 --> 00:51:09.810
part of financial services.

00:51:09.810 --> 00:51:11.970
The financial bit might
not change that much.

00:51:11.970 --> 00:51:14.640
We have lots of perspiration to
get the financial services out

00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:18.270
of the old systems into APIs.

00:51:18.270 --> 00:51:22.335
But then more broadly, see
it as a huge opportunity, OK.

00:51:22.335 --> 00:51:24.210
There's going to be 10
times as many customer

00:51:24.210 --> 00:51:28.740
journeys we can be in if we've
API products that can connect.

00:51:28.740 --> 00:51:32.130
So I don't think being
CIO of a traditional bank

00:51:32.130 --> 00:51:34.380
with old systems is an easy job.

00:51:34.380 --> 00:51:38.280
It can be made easier if all
of these belief system issues

00:51:38.280 --> 00:51:41.440
are surfaced and discussed at
the top of the organization.

00:51:41.440 --> 00:51:44.280
And then once-- in
any organization,

00:51:44.280 --> 00:51:47.220
once everyone starts
adopting the same mindset

00:51:47.220 --> 00:51:50.160
and going in the same
direction, any organization

00:51:50.160 --> 00:51:52.980
can produce phenomenal outcomes.

00:51:52.980 --> 00:51:54.030
OLIVER OGG: Great.

00:51:54.030 --> 00:51:56.697
We're coming close to the end of
the time for the fireside chat.

00:51:56.697 --> 00:51:59.280
We'll obviously have audience
questions in a moment.

00:51:59.280 --> 00:52:03.600
You mentioned the constrained
issuing of licenses.

00:52:03.600 --> 00:52:07.782
But I guess that probably
banks have been--

00:52:07.782 --> 00:52:09.240
maybe complacent
is the wrong word.

00:52:09.240 --> 00:52:11.890
But there's been the
safety net of that.

00:52:11.890 --> 00:52:14.610
But obviously that has
changed to a where, right?

00:52:14.610 --> 00:52:16.795
There's been more
that's been issued.

00:52:16.795 --> 00:52:19.170
And it kind of ties into a
question really around a trust

00:52:19.170 --> 00:52:19.670
as well.

00:52:19.670 --> 00:52:25.050
So there's a safety net of this
very valuable concept of trust.

00:52:25.050 --> 00:52:29.460
How much can banks rely
on the existing license

00:52:29.460 --> 00:52:35.190
world, the regulatory license
world, and their perception

00:52:35.190 --> 00:52:39.493
of trust in the marketplace to
protect them from disruption?

00:52:39.493 --> 00:52:41.160
PAUL ROHAN: I think
they worry too much,

00:52:41.160 --> 00:52:44.190
because many of the new
licenses that have been issued

00:52:44.190 --> 00:52:46.650
are to initiate
financial transactions.

00:52:46.650 --> 00:52:51.180
They're not necessarily to
settle financial transactions

00:52:51.180 --> 00:52:52.590
at scale.

00:52:52.590 --> 00:52:55.770
Certainly, a license to take
deposits from the public

00:52:55.770 --> 00:53:00.030
is still something that
regulators don't hand out

00:53:00.030 --> 00:53:03.660
too easily, because a public
panic and runs on banks

00:53:03.660 --> 00:53:07.410
is something that can cause a 10
year recession in your economy

00:53:07.410 --> 00:53:09.250
with a loss of confidence.

00:53:09.250 --> 00:53:13.890
And so, yes, there's a lot
more licenses and with PSD2

00:53:13.890 --> 00:53:17.130
and open banking
initiatives to allow

00:53:17.130 --> 00:53:20.310
non-banks or more likely
regulated financial

00:53:20.310 --> 00:53:22.560
institutions to
initiate payments.

00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:24.720
But much of profitability
of the industry

00:53:24.720 --> 00:53:27.930
is driven by the ability to
take large amounts of deposits

00:53:27.930 --> 00:53:31.080
from the public,
pay 2% on average,

00:53:31.080 --> 00:53:34.530
lend back to the
public possibly to tend

00:53:34.530 --> 00:53:38.490
to be to younger businesses
and younger people at 6% or 7%

00:53:38.490 --> 00:53:41.250
because you need to cover the
small number who don't pay back

00:53:41.250 --> 00:53:44.950
their loans and other risks
in relation to that process.

00:53:44.950 --> 00:53:47.370
So in the context
of that, I don't

00:53:47.370 --> 00:53:51.720
see licenses to build a
balance sheet being thrown out

00:53:51.720 --> 00:53:53.670
too easily, because
these balance sheets--

00:53:53.670 --> 00:53:55.087
the health of these
balance sheets

00:53:55.087 --> 00:53:56.950
are connected to the
health of the economy.

00:53:56.950 --> 00:54:01.300
So I do think that banks need
to get their heads around what

00:54:01.300 --> 00:54:05.580
is initiation of payments and
the abstraction of account data

00:54:05.580 --> 00:54:08.220
and what actually is the
providing of core banking

00:54:08.220 --> 00:54:12.900
services, and then recognize
that in this evolution is

00:54:12.900 --> 00:54:15.390
opportunities for partnering
at scale that they never

00:54:15.390 --> 00:54:16.770
had before.

00:54:16.770 --> 00:54:19.380
But these opportunities
will mean nothing

00:54:19.380 --> 00:54:22.950
if they don't have API products
to make those partnerships

00:54:22.950 --> 00:54:25.070
fast, flexible, reusable.

00:54:25.070 --> 00:54:29.070
And in time, the market will
get to a new steady state

00:54:29.070 --> 00:54:32.280
with this new dominant
design of how things happen.

00:54:32.280 --> 00:54:36.570
And my personal view is that
banking will thrive and will

00:54:36.570 --> 00:54:39.450
expand because of the expanded
opportunities and ecosystems.

00:54:39.450 --> 00:54:42.420
The question is, which banks
will make the transition

00:54:42.420 --> 00:54:44.143
effectively and
which banks won't?

00:54:44.143 --> 00:54:45.060
OLIVER OGG: Fantastic.

00:54:45.060 --> 00:54:47.970
A great summary there, as well.

00:54:47.970 --> 00:54:50.803
If I just go into the
perhaps some kind of

00:54:50.803 --> 00:54:52.220
takeaways that
I've really learned

00:54:52.220 --> 00:54:53.595
from that fantastic
conversation.

00:54:53.595 --> 00:54:58.130
I think, I found that
really how we position

00:54:58.130 --> 00:54:59.900
this to be really valuable.

00:54:59.900 --> 00:55:03.980
And hopefully the audience, no
matter what industry, just kind

00:55:03.980 --> 00:55:08.150
of this a reset of the
opportunity for the industry

00:55:08.150 --> 00:55:10.200
and no doubt for your business.

00:55:10.200 --> 00:55:14.687
The positioning around the
criticality of leadership,

00:55:14.687 --> 00:55:16.520
not only just understanding
this, but really

00:55:16.520 --> 00:55:18.680
continually driving it.

00:55:18.680 --> 00:55:21.020
And that developers
are customers

00:55:21.020 --> 00:55:22.170
and should be prosperous.

00:55:22.170 --> 00:55:24.260
I love that idea.

00:55:24.260 --> 00:55:27.380
And then, I liked your idea that
with digital transformation,

00:55:27.380 --> 00:55:29.810
there's often this focus
of new things and new ways,

00:55:29.810 --> 00:55:33.140
but critically
stuffing the old ways.

00:55:33.140 --> 00:55:35.330
Don't forget that
very important point.

00:55:35.330 --> 00:55:37.042
Thanks very much, Paul.

00:55:37.042 --> 00:55:39.000
KIKI MARCONDES: All right,
thank you very much.

00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:40.790
That was really interesting.

00:55:40.790 --> 00:55:42.440
Now we're going
to take some more

00:55:42.440 --> 00:55:45.360
questions from the audience.

00:55:45.360 --> 00:55:49.160
And as a reminder,
please use the link here

00:55:49.160 --> 00:55:52.730
to ask questions or
click on the Ask Question

00:55:52.730 --> 00:55:56.430
button on your Watch page.

00:55:56.430 --> 00:56:00.480
All right, so we have
another question here.

00:56:00.480 --> 00:56:02.690
So what can other
territories who

00:56:02.690 --> 00:56:06.140
have yet to do anything
around open banking

00:56:06.140 --> 00:56:10.450
learn from what the EU
and the UK have done?

00:56:14.140 --> 00:56:16.870
PAUL ROHAN: I think
there are actually

00:56:16.870 --> 00:56:19.450
quite a distinct
difference between how

00:56:19.450 --> 00:56:24.790
the EU has approached this and
how the UK has approached it.

00:56:24.790 --> 00:56:27.958
So the UK has embarked
on this initiative, which

00:56:27.958 --> 00:56:30.250
was driven to create more
competition in retail banking

00:56:30.250 --> 00:56:33.430
before PSD2 came on the horizon.

00:56:33.430 --> 00:56:37.480
And in some ways it definitely
has an implementation body

00:56:37.480 --> 00:56:39.730
and has more precise
guidance on how

00:56:39.730 --> 00:56:41.230
services should be designed.

00:56:41.230 --> 00:56:43.300
And for countries
that are thinking

00:56:43.300 --> 00:56:45.970
about moving into a
world of open banking,

00:56:45.970 --> 00:56:48.190
the UK provides a
fantastic template

00:56:48.190 --> 00:56:51.950
that mobilizes your banking
community quite quickly.

00:56:51.950 --> 00:56:58.120
And where I think the EU is
interesting and globally is

00:56:58.120 --> 00:57:03.310
it has a huge regulatory
machine because since the 1960s,

00:57:03.310 --> 00:57:05.650
Europe has been creating
a single market.

00:57:05.650 --> 00:57:08.350
So there's the perception
that this is all new red tape.

00:57:08.350 --> 00:57:12.160
No, it's just a
centralisation of red tape.

00:57:12.160 --> 00:57:14.380
And Europe hasn't been
leader in digital.

00:57:14.380 --> 00:57:16.970
And they're working very,
very hard to make that happen.

00:57:16.970 --> 00:57:19.780
But I wouldn't look
at PSD2 in isolation.

00:57:19.780 --> 00:57:23.950
So PSD2 is looking
for API connectivity

00:57:23.950 --> 00:57:25.990
between banks and third parties.

00:57:25.990 --> 00:57:29.140
It's setting out conduct rules.

00:57:29.140 --> 00:57:32.680
But there's things like GDPR
or setting out expectations

00:57:32.680 --> 00:57:33.970
around consent.

00:57:33.970 --> 00:57:39.640
And there is European work going
on around identity, privacy.

00:57:39.640 --> 00:57:42.760
And so I think where the rest
of the world should look to

00:57:42.760 --> 00:57:46.570
is whether your
regulator is going

00:57:46.570 --> 00:57:50.300
to be very precise about rules
or whether your regulator

00:57:50.300 --> 00:57:53.290
is going to give general
guidance around principles.

00:57:53.290 --> 00:57:56.200
What this work that's
happening in Europe identifies

00:57:56.200 --> 00:57:59.320
is the world is going
incredibly digital.

00:57:59.320 --> 00:58:03.940
Organizations are going to
be connected to each other.

00:58:03.940 --> 00:58:07.090
And regulators will have
expectations of good behavior

00:58:07.090 --> 00:58:08.560
and bad behavior.

00:58:08.560 --> 00:58:11.020
And it's in the interests
of the leadership

00:58:11.020 --> 00:58:13.420
and the board of directors
of an organization

00:58:13.420 --> 00:58:16.540
to go, what are these
regulators defining

00:58:16.540 --> 00:58:19.630
as good behavior and bad
behavior in terms of conduct

00:58:19.630 --> 00:58:20.980
by enterprises?

00:58:20.980 --> 00:58:24.400
And whether we have
a regulation or not,

00:58:24.400 --> 00:58:28.270
we should take that
as our cue to protect

00:58:28.270 --> 00:58:30.880
our brand in the long term.

00:58:30.880 --> 00:58:34.540
When we write policy documents
about being in ecosystems,

00:58:34.540 --> 00:58:36.010
what is our policy
around consent?

00:58:36.010 --> 00:58:38.680
When we say to our
customers, we have partners,

00:58:38.680 --> 00:58:40.758
what do we mean by partners?

00:58:40.758 --> 00:58:43.300
When we say to our customers,
we're partnering with somebody,

00:58:43.300 --> 00:58:45.508
how do we make it absolutely
plain and simple to them

00:58:45.508 --> 00:58:48.340
that we're doing this bit,
the partner is doing that bit?

00:58:48.340 --> 00:58:51.280
We're taking proper care that
we're choosing good partners.

00:58:51.280 --> 00:58:53.170
But you still need to
read the instructions.

00:58:53.170 --> 00:58:56.860
So I think for
organizations that

00:58:56.860 --> 00:58:59.260
aren't in the
regulatory space, you

00:58:59.260 --> 00:59:02.110
have the luxury of not having
to obsess over what are they

00:59:02.110 --> 00:59:04.690
saying in Article
58, but go back

00:59:04.690 --> 00:59:06.280
to the preamble of
the documentation

00:59:06.280 --> 00:59:08.777
when they say why
they're doing it.

00:59:08.777 --> 00:59:10.360
These are the type
of outcomes that we

00:59:10.360 --> 00:59:15.438
want in terms of consumer
trust and consumer protection,

00:59:15.438 --> 00:59:17.230
and that these powerful
organizations don't

00:59:17.230 --> 00:59:18.500
abuse their position.

00:59:18.500 --> 00:59:21.340
So when you read
why they're doing it

00:59:21.340 --> 00:59:25.810
and the expectations they have
generally about conduct, that's

00:59:25.810 --> 00:59:27.760
a very useful starting
point, because one

00:59:27.760 --> 00:59:30.302
of the things that's holding up
organizations getting heavily

00:59:30.302 --> 00:59:32.947
engaged in ecosystems
is going, we

00:59:32.947 --> 00:59:35.530
don't have any policy documents
from risk department about how

00:59:35.530 --> 00:59:37.060
we should conduct ourselves.

00:59:37.060 --> 00:59:39.700
Well, copy and
paste the principles

00:59:39.700 --> 00:59:42.275
that the regulators
are espousing,

00:59:42.275 --> 00:59:44.650
and maybe be glad you don't
have to follow detailed rules

00:59:44.650 --> 00:59:45.858
that regulators have written.

00:59:47.906 --> 00:59:48.656
OLIVER OGG: Great.

00:59:48.656 --> 00:59:51.060
KIKI MARCONDES: Great.

00:59:51.060 --> 00:59:55.290
OK, so thanks so much
for the questions.

00:59:55.290 --> 00:59:59.380
I hope you found this
useful and interesting.

